# P095 research notes (running log, 2026-10-18). Backup-safe: appended as I go.
## Searches so far (WebSearch standard mode)
1. Armilla: first Lloyd's coverholder dedicated to AI liability (2024); Jan 2026 limits up to $25M/org; Feb 2026 "Vanguard AI" with Chaucer (standalone AI liability policy, Chaucer cyber/tech E&O stays primary for breach). Backed by Chaucer, Axis, Convex (Armilla site). Source: armilla.ai resources + fintech.global 2026-01-23.
2. AIUC (Artificial Intelligence Underwriting Co): $15M seed Jul 2025 (NFDG), $40M Series A (Ribbit; date unclear), AIUC-1 = SOC2-like cert std for AI agents, ~5,000 tests, insurance via Lloyd's; Lovable first coding-agent cert. Sources: coverager.com/aiuc-raises-40-million, thenextweb.com Lovable.
3. Corgi: $108M raised Jan 2026 (YC/Kindred), licensed full-stack carrier for startups; AI liability product launched 2026-05-05; $160M Series B at $1.3B (TCV); reported $4B valuation after Jul 2026 extension; >$370M raised in 2026 (insurtechinsights, pulse2). => startup-focused AI liability is CROWDED + hugely funded.
4. Testudo: standalone Lloyd's-backed gen-AI policy up to $10M, 50 states (broker-site claim, unverified funding). "Mount": nothing found.
5. Silent AI: no Lloyd's/PRA mandate found (Insurance Day: regulatory push largely absent for AI vs silent cyber 2017-2020). APRA letter 30 Apr 2026 told insurers to address AI exposures. Trade-body report 17 Jun 2026 warns of silent AI. AIUC July 2026 study: >90% of insurers' AI-agent exposure in "silent" cover (single study). => "regulator-mandated silent-AI tooling" = compliance-whiplash risk, DON'T rely on mandate.
6. COI tracking: myCOI ~ $13.7M rev (Datanyze est), 82 emp; TrustLayer small ($22M raised total); Jones no data. => COI/contract-insurance verification as standalone is a SMALL market (<$100M); kill or must be much broader.
## Adjacent-file overlap check
- P096 (captives) already has idea 7 "Broker Mutual" = freight RRG under LRRA (Vermont Act 77 eff 2026-07-01 bars RRG loans/investments to members). RRG premium ~$5.0B (Vermont 80 RRGs $2.92B = 58.6% of US 2023; ~244 RRGs). Avoid duplicating; if I use an RRG/mutual, differentiate.
7. Verisk/ISO forms (Insurance Business 2026): CG 40 47 01 26 = excludes BI/PD/personal&adv injury "arising out of" gen AI (Cov A and B; optional; occurrence + claims-made); CG 40 48 = adv injury only (Cov B); CG 35 08 = products-completed ops. Berkley, Chubb, Travelers, Berkshire Hathaway, AIG each filed ISO forms/proprietary equivalents by April; 2,369 gen-AI exclusion records in force across 49 states by mid-2026; "nationwide review of state filings found NO admitted standalone product to replace the coverage". Berkley absolute AI exclusion across D&O/E&O/fiduciary. Aon 2026 AI risk report: carriers want documented AI governance; human oversight + approval protocols -> better terms. Wording: "arising out of" broad, AI need not be proximate cause, applies even if AI used indirectly by vendors (Gallagher/Shumaker/Fenwick commentary). No CG 40 47 BI/PD buy-back endorsement found. AXA XL has cyber endorsement for gen-AI (own-model developers); Beazley/QBE AI sublimits ~10% of cyber limit.
8. FT (Oct 2025, via secondary): OpenAI ~$300M cover for emerging AI risks via Aon, "falls far short"; Aon cyber head: sector lacks capacity for AI model providers; OpenAI weighing self-insurance from ~$60B investor funds + captive; Anthropic $1.5B authors settlement funded from own resources. >60 P&C insurers filed AI exclusions for 2026; dedicated AI products top out $25-50M per insured (analysis, unverified).
9. TRIA analogy: Stetler (2025 law review) 3-tier AI insurance architecture (mandatory private liability for frontier devs, industry risk pool for recurring losses, federal reinsurance for tail), draws on Price-Anderson, TRIA, crop, flood, med-mal. arXiv 2605.18784 (May 2026) "Insurability Frontier of AI Risk": backstop solves tail feasibility but highest design burden; pooled reinsurance hard because "AI-using cedent pool" hard to define; AI cat bond lacks a trigger metric; emphasizes named provider-incident trigger + anti-selection rules. No enacted federal AI backstop.
10. Travelers v. ICS (filed 2022-07-06, C.D. Ill.): Travelers sought rescission of $1M cyber policy because ICS app said MFA required for admin/privileged access; MFA was only on firewall; app signed by CEO + network-security person (Reed Smith, carriermanagement 2022-07-12). Nothing found on AI-governance statements in applications.
11. Coalition affirmative AI endorsement dates from March 2024 (cyber; AI security event + deepfake FTF). No evidence found of telemetry-priced/usage-based AI liability (Gate Meter = whitespace by negative search; low confidence).
12. Surefire Cyber (DFIR for cyber-insurance ecosystem; $10M Series A 2022 Forgepoint; AI Advisory Board 2026-06-18). DFIR firms (Surefire, Kroll, Mandiant) will extend to AI incidents => "AI forensics" not a gap.
13. Corgi's coverage primarily underwritten by Technology Risk Retention Group, Inc. (an RRG under LRRA; no guaranty fund); new admitted carrier Corgi Insurance Co. (Aug 2026, self-storage/small biz) per IB. => "AI RRG" is taken; OpenAI-scale excess is NOT.
14. Coverwatch: flat-fee (no-commission) AI commercial broker, $4.5M pre-seed Jul 2026 (CoFound, Restive); claims 20-40% savings (own claim); clients HOAs, VC-backed tech, contractors. Embroker commission-based digital broker. => fee-only buyer agent exists at seed stage; weak traction.
15. RWI + AI: Fasken Aug 2026: AI exclusions may become recurring in RWI underwriting; early, one law-firm source.
16. CSA research note 2026-08-30 "End of Silent AI Coverage": ISO circulated 3 gen-AI endorsements since Jan 1 2026; Haynes Boone Apr 2026: some exclusions say "arising out of, or in any way involving generative AI". Underwriters writing affirmative AI converge on evidence: documented human override, human-in-the-loop inventory of consequential AI decisions, data-provenance documentation, named accountable executive. Design-industry carve-backs negotiated for supervised AI use when governance documented (tool approval, verification, client disclosure, licensed sign-off).
17. Broker E&O: Munich Re/HSB: advisory gray zone; Swiss Re 2024 (via vendor blog, unverified): inadequate coverage advice + failure to obtain required coverage >60% of paid E&O losses vs agencies; Gallagher (secondhand): ~20% of insurance professionals saw AI-related client loss/claim in past year, half paid in full.
18. Deloitte: global AI insurance premiums ~$4.7-4.8B by 2032 (~80% CAGR). Munich Re aiSure since ~2018. => affirmative-AI premium pool is small vs GL base ($131B US, derived) and cyber ($16.3B 2025 -> ~$29B 2027).
